sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'1.1'))
from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('4.1')
for local,texts in {
 '01':['Occupational choice can depend on both monetary pay and non-wage characteristics such as conditions, security, travel and development.','The most suitable offer depends on the individual’s priorities, constraints and feasible alternatives rather than the wage alone.','A non-wage feature can have later financial consequences without being identical to the current wage.'],
 '01-M01':['Wage factors include the level and reliability of monetary remuneration, such as basic pay, overtime pay or bonuses under stated terms.','Comparing pay offers requires consistent periods, hours and relevant stated work-related costs.'],
 '01-M02':['Non-wage factors include working conditions, hours, job security, travel, interest in the work and development opportunities.','Different people can assign different values to the same non-wage characteristic.'],
}.items():
 for text in texts:t.claim(local,text,'evaluative' if local=='01' else 'factual')
t.block('01','comparison','Pay is one part of an occupational choice',"""
An individual choosing an occupation considers both wage and non-wage factors. Wage factors concern monetary remuneration; non-wage factors concern characteristics such as working conditions, hours, security, travel and development. A higher advertised wage can be attractive without establishing that the job is best for every person. The conclusion depends on what the individual needs, values and can realistically do.

Consider two invented job offers in Namibia. One offers more pay but requires a long journey. Another pays less but offers useful training and a shorter journey. The choice involves a trade-off rather than an automatic ranking. Someone with an urgent need for income may give greater weight to pay; someone with caring responsibilities or a specific development goal may value time or training more. State the priority supporting a recommendation instead of assuming everyone has identical preferences. The offers must also be feasible: qualifications, location and the ability to meet the job’s stated requirements can rule out an apparent alternative before preferences are compared.
""")
t.block('01-M01','plain_explanation','Compare monetary remuneration on a common basis',"""
Wage factors include basic pay and additional monetary remuneration such as overtime pay or a bonus under stated terms. Reliability matters: an uncertain bonus is not the same as guaranteed basic pay in the supplied contract. Compare the same periods and hours before judging the financial difference. A monthly figure cannot be directly compared with an hourly rate without enough information about working time.

Work-related costs can reduce the practical financial advantage of a higher pay offer. Use invented monthly figures with other deductions ignored. Job A pays N$8,000 and requires N$800 travel expenditure; Job B pays N$7,200 and requires N$200. Pay remaining after these stated travel costs is N$7,200 for A and N$7,000 for B. A still offers more on this measure, but its advantage is N$200 rather than the N$800 difference in advertised pay.

These remaining amounts are not a complete disposable-income calculation: taxes or other deductions would need separate facts. Likewise, travel cost does not put a monetary value on the journey time or discomfort. The comparison shows why the relevant financial evidence should be established before a wider judgement. If the individual strongly values an additional N$200 and can manage the other conditions, A’s monetary advantage can matter. If that amount is less important than the time lost, the higher wage may not decide the choice.
""")
t.block('01-M02','plain_explanation','Non-wage features can change the attraction of a job',"""
Working conditions include the physical environment, safety arrangements, workload and relationships at work. Hours and location affect how the job fits other responsibilities. Security concerns the reliability of continuing work, while interest and satisfaction concern the activity itself. Training and promotion opportunities can affect future development. These features matter even when the immediate money wage is unchanged.

Suppose Job A involves two hours of total travel each working day and Job B half an hour. B saves one and a half hours per day. A worker who needs that time for family responsibilities can reasonably prefer B, provided its pay meets the worker’s essential needs. Another worker may place less value on that time and prefer A’s financial advantage. Neither preference is established as irrational merely because the nominal pay ranking differs.

Training can also connect non-wage and financial considerations. An opportunity to learn a skill is a development feature of the job, while future higher earnings are a possible consequence. Do not promise promotion or a wage rise solely because training is offered. Its usefulness depends on quality, relevance and later opportunities. Explain the feature and the link to the individual’s goal rather than describing a lower-paid job as automatically superior because it includes a course.
""")
t.block('01','evaluation','Reach a conditional choice',"""
Start a comparison with the individual’s stated objective, then identify the relevant advantages and sacrifices. In the invented offers, A provides N$200 more per month after the stated travel costs, while B saves one and a half hours each working day and offers training. If immediate additional income is decisive and the long journey is feasible, A can be justified. If both offers meet essential income needs and caring time or relevant training is more important, B can be justified.

The condition that changes the judgement should be explicit. An uncertain training promise, a changed travel cost or a new family responsibility can alter the comparison. This is the same opportunity-cost reasoning used in topic 1.1: the chosen benefit is assessed against the best feasible alternative forgone. Topic 4.2 explains earnings differences, while the present topic owns the individual’s choice between occupations and employment conditions.
""")
def s(local,sub):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['objective_id']==t.oid(local) and x['subtype']==sub)
t.card(s('01','DIST'),'01','Explain the distinction between wage and non-wage factors in choosing an occupation.','Wage factors concern monetary remuneration; non-wage factors concern features such as hours, conditions, security or development rather than the immediate money wage.',['Difference (1): monetary remuneration. Contrast/test (1): job characteristics distinct from immediate pay.'])
t.card(s('01','APP'),'01','Explain why a worker might choose a lower-paid offer with a shorter journey when both offers meet essential income needs and caring time is the priority.','The shorter journey releases time for caring, a valued non-wage benefit. With essential income already met, that benefit can outweigh the extra pay forgone.',['Context (1): priority for caring and shorter journey. Reason/conclusion (1): non-wage time benefit outweighs additional pay under the supplied constraint.'])
t.card(s('01-M01','DIST'),'01-M01','Explain the difference between basic pay and journey time as factors in an occupational choice.','Basic pay is monetary remuneration, a wage factor. Journey time is a non-wage characteristic affecting the time cost and convenience of the job.',['Difference (1): basic pay is monetary. Contrast (1): journey time is a non-wage characteristic.'])
t.card(s('01-M01','APP'),'01-M01','Explain the monetary comparison using invented monthly offers with other deductions ignored: A pays N$8,000 with N$800 travel cost; B pays N$7,200 with N$200 travel cost.','After the stated travel expenditure, A leaves N$7,200 and B N$7,000. A’s financial advantage is N$200 on this measure, not the N$800 difference in headline pay.',['Context (1): account for stated travel costs. Reason/conclusion (1): compare N$7,200 versus N$7,000, a N$200 advantage.'])
t.card(s('01-M02','DIST'),'01-M02','Explain the distinction between training opportunities and an overtime payment in an occupational choice.','Training is a development characteristic, a non-wage feature. The overtime payment is monetary remuneration for stated work, a wage factor.',['Difference (1): training/development feature. Contrast (1): overtime money is remuneration.'])
t.card(s('01-M02','APP'),'01-M02','Explain one non-wage reason to prefer a job offering relevant training when immediate pay is sufficient but developing a skill is the individual’s priority.','Relevant training supports the stated development goal, so it can make the job more attractive without requiring a higher immediate wage; later promotion is not guaranteed.',['Context (1): relevant training matches development priority. Reason/consequence (1): non-wage development benefit supports choice, without guaranteeing promotion.'])
base='Invented Namibian monthly offers, with other deductions ignored: A pays N$8,000, travel costs N$800 and total daily travel takes two hours; B pays N$7,200, travel costs N$200 and travel takes half an hour. Working hours are otherwise the same. B offers relevant training.'
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][0]
t.card(p,'01-M01 01-M02 01','Identify two wage factors in occupational choice (2). Identify two non-wage factors (2). Explain why safer working conditions can affect a supplied choice where safety is the worker’s priority and pay is adequate (2). Identify a wage and a non-wage feature from this invented offer: monthly basic pay and relevant training (2).','Basic pay and an overtime payment are wage factors. Journey time and safety conditions are non-wage factors. Safer conditions address the worker’s stated priority and can outweigh additional pay if adequate income is available. The offer’s basic pay is wage-related and training non-wage development.',['Identification, separate points (2): basic pay (1), overtime pay (1). Non-wage points (2): journey time (1), safety (1). Explanation (2): safer conditions match priority (1), can determine choice when pay is adequate (1). Final features (2): basic pay wage (1), training non-wage (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][1]
t.card(p,'01-M01 01-M02 01',base+' Describe three differences relevant to the choice (6). Explain how money remaining after travel and non-wage features can change the headline-pay comparison (7). Discuss which offer suits someone prioritising caring time and skill development, with essential income needs met by either offer (7).','A pays more, has greater travel cost and requires a longer journey; B also offers training. After stated travel expenditure A leaves N$7,200 and B N$7,000, narrowing the monetary difference to N$200. B saves one and a half hours each working day and offers a relevant development opportunity, without guaranteed promotion. B better serves the stated caring/development priorities if its pay remains adequate and training is useful. A retains a financial advantage, so a change in urgent income needs could alter the conclusion.',['Context/data description (6): pay difference (1), N$8,000/N$7,200 evidence (1); travel cost difference (1), N$800/N$200 evidence (1); travel time difference (1), two hours/half-hour evidence (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): A remaining N$7,200 (1), B remaining N$7,000 (1), gap only N$200 (1); B saves 1.5 hours (1), supporting caring (1); training supports development (1), not guaranteed promotion (1). Both sides/judgement (7): A offers extra money (1), may help income needs (1); B offers time (1), development (1); condition adequate income/useful training (1); choose B for stated priority (1), reconsider if financial constraint changes (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][2]
t.card(p,'01',base+' Evaluate the choice in two seven-mark plans. Case 1: an individual urgently values the extra money and can manage the longer journey. Case 2: either offer meets essential income needs, while caring time and useful training are decisive. Include opposing arguments and the condition supporting each judgement.','Case 1: A’s N$200 extra after the stated travel cost supports the urgent income priority, but longer travel sacrifices time and B’s training is forgone. Choose A if the extra income matters more and travel is manageable. Case 2: B saves 1.5 daily hours and offers training, but gives up N$200 on the stated measure. Choose B if income remains adequate and training is relevant; no guaranteed future promotion should be assumed.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for more remaining income (1), meets urgent need (1); argument against longer travel (1), forgone development (1); condition manageable journey and stronger income priority (1); judgement A (1) tied to that condition (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for time saving (1), relevant training (1); argument against less remaining income (1), development outcome uncertain (1); condition adequate income and useful training (1); judgement B (1) tied to the stated priorities (1). Total 14.'],[base])
assert 8000-800==7200 and 7200-200==7000 and 7200-7000==200
